
// Base Omega Library
//
// Doc convention:
//   - `///` lines are public docs — extracted into the AI preamble
//     (common/program/preamble/libraries.go) and shown in the Libraries
//     panel. Write them for users.
//   - Plain `//` lines are internal notes — they stay in the source
//     for library authors but never reach the preamble. Use them for
//     implementation details, attributions, TODOs, etc.
//   - A blank line ends a doc block; a `//` line in the middle of one
//     is ignored (doesn't break or appear).

// The transform helpers (rotateXY, eulerAngles, patternPolarXY, ...) live in
// the `map` package, and builtin does not import it: the one internal rotation
// (in arcTwoPointTangentExtendedXY) is inlined below instead. The constant
// vectors (zero, dirX, dirY, dirZ) live in the `vector` package, which 3d.gsl
// imports for its axis-aligned primitives — mutual dependency with a library
// that dot-imports builtin is fine, but keep it to that one case.

// =========================================================
// CONSTANTS
// =========================================================
pi = atan(1) * 4;
tau = pi * 2;
sqrt2 = sqrt(2);
sqrt3 = sqrt(3);
e = exp(1);

/// Millimeters are the unit; these convert into them. Write 2.0 * inch rather
/// than 50.8, so the drawing's number stays visible in the source. `in` is a
/// reserved word, hence `inch`, and there is no `mm` because 1.0 * mm says
/// nothing that 1.0 does not.
inch = 25.4;
/// Feet to millimeters.
foot = 304.8;
/// Meters to millimeters.
meter = 1000.0;

/// The empty solid: far from everything, so min(d, nothing) and
/// max(d, -nothing) are just d. It is finite on purpose: the mesher lowers
/// c ? a : b as c * a + (1 - c) * b, which drops the branch not taken by
/// multiplying it by 0, and 0 times infinity is not 0.
nothing = 1e5;

/// How far past a declared name.box its crop reaches: ten times the
/// raycaster's hit floor, so a box computed from a part's nominal dimensions
/// never shaves float-level overshoot off a face that touches it.
boxPad = 0.01;

/// First element of a list. A scalar behaves as a list of length one, so
/// firstOf(x) == x — handy for arguments that broadcast a single value or
/// accept a per-slot list. Pairs with lastOf().
// The no-initial reduce seeds the accumulator with the first element, so
// keeping it yields the first and overwriting it yields the last.
firstOf(list) {
  return reduce((first, element) {
    return first;
  }, list);
}

/// Last element of a list. A scalar behaves as a list of length one, so
/// lastOf(x) == x. Pairs with firstOf() for broadcast-or-per-slot arguments.
lastOf(list) {
  return reduce((previous, element) {
    return element;
  }, list);
}

// Internal: a value that draws as `stand` when its part is small on screen: a
// colour or material's name.simple = stand, a texture's flat look for one
// (property.Expand, property.MarkParts). It is the value wherever it is built;
// the viewer's WGSL draws the stand-in instead, without evaluating the value,
// below the Detail slider's Textures rung, a colour's and a material's alike,
// blending across the octave below it (agent/wgsl/internal/codegen/simple.go).
simpleValue(value, stand) {
  return value;
}

// Internal: a value with fine detail, name.detail = fine, of features
// name.detailSize = size mm across (0.15, wood's fibre, without one), its
// coarse look otherwise (property.Expand). The viewer's WGSL draws fine where
// a pixel is under 0.5 of the size, where fadeDetail(size) is above 0, and
// coarse elsewhere, without evaluating fine; the Render panel's Pixel filter
// scales the pixel (agent/wgsl/internal/codegen/simple.go). Fine detail fades
// to its coarse look by that pixel, as fadeDetail(size) fades it, so fine is
// the value wherever it is built, as simpleValue's is.
detailValue(coarse, fine, size) {
  return fine;
}

// Internal: simpleValue for a stand-in that has a stand-in of its own, a
// simpler texture before the flat look (property.MarkParts). The viewer
// switches both below the Textures rung, so the simpler texture shows only
// while the value blends to the flat look.
simpleTexture(value, stand) {
  return value;
}
